<h1>Privacy</h1>
<p class="lede">AlarmSwap is a set of plain reference pages. It doesn't ask who you are, and it keeps as little about your visit as we could manage.</p>
<p class="last-checked">Updated <time datetime="2026-11-19">November 19, 2026</time></p>

<h2>Hosting</h2>
<p>The site is static files served by <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/what-is-github-pages" rel="nofollow noopener">GitHub Pages</a>. GitHub says it logs and stores the IP address of anyone who visits a GitHub Pages site, for security purposes. That log is GitHub's, under the <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="nofollow noopener">GitHub Privacy Statement</a>; we don't get a copy.</p>

<h2>Visit counts</h2>
<p>We count page views with Cloudflare Web Analytics, a small script loaded from Cloudflare on each page. It is cookieless and doesn't track individuals. Cloudflare's documentation says Web Analytics <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/about/" rel="nofollow noopener">does not collect or use your visitors' personal data</a>, and its product page says it <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" rel="nofollow noopener">uses no client-side state such as cookies or localStorage</a> to collect those numbers. What we see are totals: page views, the pages people read, the sites that linked here, and how fast pages loaded. We can't see who you are.</p>

<h2>Search</h2>
<p>The search box uses <a href="https://pagefind.app/" rel="nofollow noopener">Pagefind</a>. It runs in your browser and looks things up in index files served from this site, so what you type is not sent to us or to a search company.</p>

<h2>No forms, accounts or cookies of our own</h2>
<p>There is nothing to sign up for and no form to fill in. The site itself sets no cookies.</p>

<h2>Links to other sites</h2>
<p>Citations link to alarm makers, NFPA, USFA and CPSC, which have their own privacy policies. Some pages link to Amazon. As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. If you click an outbound retailer or affiliate link, that retailer (for example Amazon or Walmart) or its affiliate network may set its own cookies to credit the referral, under that retailer's own privacy policy. The <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a> explains those links.</p>

